        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is running on a treadmill easier than running outside?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually slightly, at the same pace. The main reason is that there is no air resistance on a treadmill, and the surface is flat and uniform. The difference is small at easy paces and larger at faster ones. Many runners find treadmill runs feel harder anyway, because of the heat of an indoor room with no airflow and the monotony of staying in one place.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I set my treadmill to 1% incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a sensible default if you want faster running indoors to resemble the effort of outdoor running. The convention comes from a commonly cited 1996 study by Jones and Doust, which found a 1% grade best reflected outdoor energy cost at the moderate-to-faster paces tested. At easy paces it makes little difference, and it does no harm either way.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill mile the same as an outdoor mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Close, but not identical. Without air resistance a treadmill mile at the same pace costs slightly less energy, especially at faster speeds, and treadmill speed displays are not precisely calibrated. As training, a treadmill mile is entirely real. For pace comparisons, treat the treadmill as consistent with itself rather than exactly matched to the road.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why do I get so hot running on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Because there is no airflow. Outdoors, air moving past you carries heat away and evaporates sweat; on a treadmill you stay still relative to the room, which is often warm already. A standalone fan aimed at your upper body, a cool room and water to hand make a large difference. Built-in treadmill fans are generally too weak to help much.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I train for a race only on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Many people do much of their training indoors, but some race skills are hard to build on a belt: downhill running, turns, uneven ground, self-pacing and running in wind or heat. Most runners find it worth including some outdoor running before an outdoor race. For a specific plan, ask a qualified coach who can see your training; a general guide like this cannot write an individual plan.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does a treadmill change your running form?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A little, mostly at first. Runners new to treadmills often take shorter, quicker strides and hold the rails or look down, and most settle close to their outdoor stride after a few sessions. A deck long enough for your stride, looking ahead, staying off the rails and running near the middle of the belt all help. Research generally finds the differences small.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

